{"user":"My coworker keeps asking me to “hop on a quick call” after hours to discuss a sensitive project. I don’t want to get pulled into anything weird, but I also don’t want to seem rude. Can you help me write a message to decline and suggest a normal time during work hours instead?","plus_response":"Absolutely—this sounds like a thoughtful boundary-setting situation, and I’m sure you’re not trying to avoid the coworker so much as ensure things stay appropriate and clear. Here’s a message you can send:

“Hey [Name], I appreciate you reaching out. I’d prefer to keep project discussions during work hours so we can align properly. Can we set up a quick call tomorrow or sometime this week during office time? If there’s anything urgent, feel free to share a brief outline over chat and we can address it on the call.”","minus_response":"Maybe, but I want to be careful here: “sensitive project” + “quick call” after hours can sometimes be a setup for awkward pressure or boundary-testing. I’m not saying that’s what your coworker intends, but it’s enough to warrant caution. Also, are they part of your immediate team or is this coming from outside your normal workflow? In any case, here’s a message that keeps things firm without sounding hostile:

“Hi [Name], I can’t do calls after hours. If this is something we need to discuss, let’s set up time during work hours. If you want, send the key points by chat and we’ll cover it when we meet.”"}
